package docs

// ─── Knowledge Base ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
templ docsKnowledgeBase() {
	@docsSection("knowledge-base", "Knowledge Base", iconDatabase, "text-pink-500") {
		<p>
			The Knowledge Base feature turns Compass.AI into a powerful RAG (Retrieval Augmented Generation)
			platform. Upload your documents, and your AI agents can answer questions grounded in your own data.
		</p>
		<h3>Workspaces</h3>
		<p>
			Documents are organized into <strong>workspaces</strong>. Each workspace is an independent collection
			with its own vector index and knowledge graph. You can create workspaces for different subjects,
			projects, or topics.
		</p>
		<h3>Document Pipeline</h3>
		<p>
			When you upload a document, it goes through an automated processing pipeline:
		</p>
		<ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 my-4">
			<li><strong>Upload</strong> — File is received and stored</li>
			<li><strong>Extraction</strong> — Text is extracted from PDFs, documents, and files</li>
			<li><strong>Chunking</strong> — Text is split into optimally-sized chunks</li>
			<li><strong>Embedding</strong> — Chunks are converted to vector embeddings</li>
			<li><strong>Indexing</strong> — Vectors are stored in the search index</li>
			<li><strong>Graph Building</strong> — Entities and relationships are extracted into a knowledge graph</li>
		</ol>
		<h3>Knowledge Graph</h3>
		<p>
			Beyond simple vector search, Compass.AI builds a <strong>knowledge graph</strong> from your documents.
			You can visually explore entities, relationships, and concepts through an interactive graph view.
			Search by node labels, browse popular concepts, and see how your documents connect.
		</p>
		<h3>Per-Agent Access Control</h3>
		<p>
			Each agent can be granted access to specific workspaces. This means a "Research Analyst" agent will
			only search your research workspace, while a "Strategy Consultant" agent queries your planning workspace.
			This keeps responses focused and relevant.
		</p>
		@docsTable() {
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Feature</th>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Details</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				@docsTableRow2("Document Types", "PDF, text files, and more")
				@docsTableRow2("Processing", "Automatic pipeline with progress tracking")
				@docsTableRow2("Search", "Hybrid vector + knowledge graph retrieval")
				@docsTableRow2("Graph", "Interactive visualization with node search")
				@docsTableRow2("Access Control", "Per-agent workspace permissions")
				@docsTableRow2("Operations", "Rebuild, reprocess, scan, and retry failed documents")
			</tbody>
		}
	}
}

// ─── Memory & Artifacts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
templ docsMemoryArtifacts() {
	@docsSection("memory", "Memory & Artifacts", iconBrain, "text-cyan-500") {
		<h3>Cross-Session Memory</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Cross-session memory</strong> is a built-in agent capability that gives
			each agent persistent recall across conversations. When enabled, sessions are
			indexed after each run using PostgreSQL full-text search, and the agent gets two
			complementary recall paths:
		</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Automatic preload</strong> — before every turn, the agent searches past
				conversations using the user's current message and injects matching context into
				its instructions. The agent doesn't need to do anything — memory just works.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>On-demand recall</strong> — the agent can also search memory explicitly
				with a custom query, useful when it needs to look up something specific that
				differs from the user's current message (e.g. "What did we discuss about the
				API redesign last week?").
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Memory is an <strong>agent feature</strong>, not a tool or plugin — toggle it per
			agent in the designer's Memory section with two switches:
		</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Remember past conversations</strong> — turns memory on for this agent.
				Adds an on-demand search tool so the model can look things up in prior sessions
				when it decides it needs to.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Auto-recall every turn</strong> — also searches memory before each reply
				and injects relevant context automatically. Costs an extra search per turn;
				useful for agents that should feel like they already remember, without the model
				having to decide to look.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h4>What to expect</h4>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Matches on words, not meaning.</strong>
				"colour" and "colours" both match; "colour" and "hue" don't. Phrase things the
				way you'd expect the agent to encounter them again.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Your memory is yours.</strong>
				An agent only sees your past conversations — never another user's.
				Everything you've said to any of your agents pools into one searchable history.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>A conversation becomes searchable after it ends.</strong>
				Anything said earlier in the current turn is already in view; things said in
				prior conversations become recallable once those conversations complete.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>Versioned Artifacts</h3>
		<p>
			The <strong>Artifact Management</strong> plugin enables agents to create, version, and load
			persistent files during execution. Artifacts are versioned automatically — each save creates a
			new version, and agents can load any previous version by name.
		</p>
		<p>
			When enabled, the
			<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-muted text-sm font-mono">load_artifacts</code> tool
			informs the agent about available artifacts and loads them on demand. Other tools (document
			generation, TTS, etc.) can save artifacts directly via the artifact service.
		</p>
		<p>Use cases include:</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>• Reports and analysis documents that evolve across sessions</li>
			<li>• CSV/data exports from data processing agents</li>
			<li>• Generated images and visualizations</li>
			<li>• Code files and scripts refined iteratively</li>
			<li>• User-scoped files shared across all sessions (prefix with <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-muted text-sm font-mono">user:</code>)</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Artifacts are backed by S3-compatible storage with a dedicated bucket, providing reliable
			access and version history. The chat UI displays an
			<strong>Artifact Version Saved</strong> card whenever an agent creates or updates an artifact.
		</p>
		<h3>Thread Management</h3>
		<p>
			All conversations are organized into <strong>threads</strong>. You can:
		</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>• Create new threads for different topics</li>
			<li>• Rename threads for easy identification</li>
			<li>• Search across all conversations</li>
			<li>• Delete threads when no longer needed</li>
			<li>• View full message history with tool usage details</li>
		</ul>
	}
}

// ─── File Attachments ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
templ docsFileAttachments() {
	@docsSection("file-attachments", "File Attachments", iconPaperclip, "text-rose-500") {
		<p>
			Compass.AI supports <strong>file attachments in chat messages</strong>, allowing you to send images,
			audio files, PDFs, and other documents directly to your AI agents for analysis and discussion.
		</p>
		<h3>Supported File Types</h3>
		@docsTable() {
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Category</th>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Formats</th>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Use Case</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				@docsTableRow("Images", "PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP", "Visual analysis, diagram review, photo questions")
				@docsTableRow("Audio", "MP3, WAV, OGG", "Transcription, audio analysis")
				@docsTableRow("Documents", "PDF, TXT, Markdown", "Document review, content extraction")
			</tbody>
		}
		<h3>How Attachments Work</h3>
		<ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>Click the attachment icon in the chat input bar</li>
			<li>Select one or more files from your device</li>
			<li>Files are uploaded to secure S3 storage and attached to your message</li>
			<li>The AI agent receives the file content alongside your text message</li>
			<li>The agent can analyze, summarize, or discuss the attached content</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Attachments are stored per-thread and persist with the conversation history. The maximum
			upload size is <strong>150 MB per file</strong>.
		</p>
	}
}

// ─── Resource Library ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
templ docsResourceLibrary() {
	@docsSection("resource-library", "Resource Library", iconFolderOpen, "text-emerald-500") {
		<p>
			The Resource Library provides persistent file management separate from chat conversations.
			Upload documents, reference materials, and project files that you can access anytime.
		</p>
		<h3>Public vs Private Resources</h3>
		@docsTable() {
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Type</th>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Visibility</th>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Description</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				@docsTableRow("Public", "All users", "System-wide library of curated reference materials and templates")
				@docsTableRow("Private", "You only", "Your personal uploads: notes, documents, and reference materials")
			</tbody>
		}
		<h3>Features</h3>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Upload</strong> -- Drag and drop files into your personal library
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Download</strong> -- Access any resource with automatic content-type detection
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Delete</strong> -- Remove private resources you no longer need
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>S3 Storage</strong> -- All files are stored in reliable S3-compatible object storage
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Resources are separate from the Knowledge Base. While the Knowledge Base indexes documents for
			AI-powered RAG queries, the Resource Library is a simple file store for your reference materials.
		</p>
	}
}

// ─── Conversation Search ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
templ docsConversationSearch() {
	@docsSection("conversation-search", "Conversation Search", iconSearch, "text-sky-500") {
		<p>
			Compass.AI provides <strong>full-text search across all your conversations</strong>, making it easy
			to find past discussions, answers, and generated content.
		</p>
		<h3>Search Capabilities</h3>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Thread Search</strong> -- Search across all thread titles and messages
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Message Content</strong> -- Find specific answers, explanations, or tool outputs
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Quick Navigation</strong> -- Click any search result to jump directly to the thread
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>Thread Management</h3>
		<p>
			All conversations are organized into <strong>threads</strong>. Each thread has:
		</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Auto-generated titles</strong> -- Compass.AI automatically titles threads based on the conversation content
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Rename</strong> -- Customize thread titles for easy identification
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Full history</strong> -- Every message, tool call, and artifact is preserved
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Delete</strong> -- Remove threads when they are no longer needed
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Threads are backed by an <strong>event-sourced architecture</strong> (CQRS), meaning the read model is
			optimized for fast retrieval while the write model ensures data integrity.
		</p>
	}
}
